{{/if}}

## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the simulation being deterministic and steppable. Three
properties make it so.

- A fixed timestep, decoupled from rendering, as `specs/overview.md` fixes it.
- Simulation-owned contacts. Every contact is decided from the state's own polar
  figures under the crossing rules of `specs/deflector-and-ball.md` and
  `specs/rings.md`, so what a tick resolves follows from the state alone.
- Seeded randomness. The game holds one pseudo-random generator, seeded by
  `reset` and consumed only by the pod draws `specs/pods.md` states, so
  reseeding and replaying the same calls reproduces the same pod sequence
  exactly.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of operations and elapsed ticks, the
game reproduces identical snapshots every time.

Audio belongs to the ticks. A pose changes the state alone and sounds nothing;
the cues a scenario hears come from the ticks run after it.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard, the pointer, and the overlay belong to the runtime layer beneath
the game, and the surface carries no operation for any of them. That layer
delivers the input `specs/controls.md` fixes, so a dispatched keyboard event
turns the deflector, launches the ball, and works the menus exactly as a
player's key does, and a dispatched pointer event works the menus exactly as a
player's mouse or finger does, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.

The clock is the exception, because nothing outside this build owns it. The
surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through
`setAutoStep` and `step` below.
{{else}}
The clock, the input, and the overlay belong to the {{engine.name}} engine,
which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository,
and the surface carries no operation for any of them. The engine advances the
game frame by frame, and a clock that supplies its own deltas takes it off real
time: a scenario pairs a `ConstantClock` of `1000 / 60` milliseconds with
`engine.advance`, so one frame consumes exactly one tick. The engine drives the
actions the game registers and the pointer it maps, so a dispatched keyboard
event turns the deflector, launches the ball, and works the menus exactly as a
player's key does, a dispatched pointer event works the menus exactly as a
player's mouse or finger does, and the engine draws and toggles the debug
overlay.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Kessler's, under Diagnostics below.

## The driver switches

Two switches give a scenario a hold on the game's two autonomous consequences,
so it can watch one behavior without another arriving on top of it. Each is on
when the game is played, each is restored by `reset`, each is set by its own
operation below, and each is reported by the snapshot.

| Switch | While it is on | While it is off |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `waveAdvance` | A ball-caused destruction that leaves zero live targets is the clearing event: the field empties, the interstitial runs, and the next wave begins, as `specs/rings.md` states. | The clearing event does not fire. Play carries on in `playing` over an empty field, and destructions keep scoring. |
| `podSpawn` | Each destruction makes the pod draw `specs/pods.md` states, consuming the generator. | No draw is made and the generator is not consumed, so no pod spawns and the seeded sequence stays where it stands. |

A switch changes only what it names. Turning one back on resumes that
consequence from the next event onward, with no catching up for the events it
missed.

## The operations

The surface carries the operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings, and
booleans. Positions and velocities are in the stage's logical units and logical
units per second, angles are in degrees under the polar mapping
`specs/field.md` fixes, and durations are in whole ticks.

Each pose sets one thing and leaves the rest of the game as it stands, so a
caller that wants several things arranged makes several calls, in the order it
wants them. No pose decides an outcome: every bounce, hit, destruction, catch,
burn-up, life loss, and clearing comes from the ticks run after the pose, so a
scenario reads what happened from the snapshot.

`paddle` names the deflector throughout the operations and the snapshot below.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}

Each operation's first parameter is the current state,
`DeepReadonly<KesslerState>`. A pose returns the next `KesslerState`, built from
the one it was handed, which it leaves as it was; a reading returns what it
read. The headings below name the parameters that follow the state.
{{/if}}

An argument outside the domain its operation states is invalid, and the call
fails loudly rather than guessing what was meant, except where an operation
states that it normalizes or ignores the call.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setAutoStep(auto)`

Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. `setAutoStep(false)` stops the
frame loop feeding the wall clock's delta time into the tick accumulator, so no
tick runs until `step` runs one. `setAutoStep(true)` returns the game to
advancing itself, which is how a build starts and how it is played. It changes
no game state.

Drawing and input are unaffected either way: the loop keeps rendering and keeps
reading the keys, so a menu still answers a key press while the simulation is
held. The snapshot reports the switch as `autoStep`.

### `step(ticks)`

Runs `ticks` whole simulation ticks, immediately and in order, each the full
tick of `specs/field.md` followed by a render, so contacts resolve, cues fire,
and the canvas reflects the result. `ticks` is a whole number of at least `1`
and defaults to `1`.

Stepping while the game is still stepping automatically adds to what the wall
clock is already doing, so a reproducible scenario calls `setAutoStep(false)`
first.

{{/if}}
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `reset(state, seed)`
{{else}}
### `reset(seed)`
{{/if}}

Restores the game to its boot state: the `title` screen
with `menu.index` at `0`, the score at `0`, `3` lives, wave `1`, `ticks` at `0`,
the deflector at angle `90` with span `48`, every ring slot filled at its full
hit points with every ring angle at `0` and each ring's speed from the wave-1
formulas, no balls, no pods, no timed effect, no shield, the interstitial timer
at `0`, and both driver switches on. `seed` is an optional whole number that
seeds the pod generator, defaulting to {{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}`1`{{else}}`DEFAULT_SEED` (`1`){{/if}}.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}

Whether the simulation advances on its own each frame is untouched.
`setAutoStep` belongs to the caller driving the game rather than to the session
being played, so a `reset` inside a stepped scenario leaves the game off the
wall clock and the scenario stays reproducible.
{{/if}}

A reset leaves the game indistinguishable from a freshly started session, so
anything else the build keeps across frames is derived from what a reset
restores.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `snapshot(state)`

A pure reading of `state`, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape
below. It returns no state, because it poses nothing.

### `menuItemRect(state, index)`
{{else}}
### `snapshot()`

A pure read of the state, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape
below. It changes nothing.

### `menuItemRect(index)`
{{/if}}

A pure read of where the build drew a menu entry. It returns the hit region of
entry `index` on the current screen's menu, as
`{ x: <number>, y: <number>, width: <number>, height: <number> }`, in the
stage's logical units, with `(x, y)` the region's top-left corner. It is the
region `specs/controls.md` has the pointer and touch act over, so what it
reports is where the entry actually answers. On a screen with no menu, and for
an `index` outside the menu's entries, it returns `null`. It changes nothing.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setScreen(state, name)`
{{else}}
### `setScreen(name)`
{{/if}}

Sets `screen` to `name`, one of the six values `specs/screens.md` names, and
changes nothing else. The score, the lives, the wave, the deflector, the balls,
the rings, the pods, the timed effects, the shield, the interstitial timer, the
menu highlight, and both driver switches all stand exactly as they stood. No
cue sounds at the call, as the deterministic core above states.

What each screen then does is the screen's own, fixed in `specs/screens.md`: a
tick of `playing` advances the whole simulation, a tick of `waveclear` counts
the interstitial timer down, and `title`, `howto`, `paused`, and `gameover`
advance nothing. A caller that wants a screen arranged the way the real
transition into it arranges it makes the calls that arrange it.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setScore(state, n)`, `setLives(state, n)`
{{else}}
### `setScore(n)`, `setLives(n)`
{{/if}}

Set the score, and the lives, to `n`, a whole number of at least `0`. Play
carries on from the posed figure: the next scoring event adds to the posed
score, and a life loss at posed lives `1` ends the game.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setMenuIndex(state, n)`
{{else}}
### `setMenuIndex(n)`
{{/if}}

Sets the highlighted menu entry to `n`, a whole number from `0` to the entry
count of the current screen's menu minus `1`. The highlight moves exactly as
`up` and `down` move it, and no cue sounds. On a screen with no menu the call
changes nothing.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setInterstitialTicks(state, ticks)`
{{else}}
### `setInterstitialTicks(ticks)`
{{/if}}

Sets the interstitial timer to `ticks`, a whole number of at least `0`. The
timer counts down only on `waveclear`, where each tick lowers it by `1` and the
tick that takes it to `0` ends the interstitial, laying the next wave out and
returning `screen` to `playing` as `specs/screens.md` states; a tick of
`waveclear` with the timer already at `0` ends it at once. On every other
screen the posed figure simply holds. The clearing event sets it to `180`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setWave(state, n)`
{{else}}
### `setWave(n)`
{{/if}}

Sets the wave counter to `n`, a whole number of at least `1`, and puts the
wave-`n` figures in force: each ring's speed is set from the wave formulas of
`specs/rings.md`, overwriting any `setRingSpeed`, and the wave ball speed used
by a serve, a launch, and a deflector bounce becomes the wave-`n` figure of
`specs/deflector-and-ball.md`. The rings keep their targets and their angles,
every ball keeps the velocity it holds, and the effects, pods, score, lives,
and screen stay as they stand.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setPaddleAngle(state, deg)`
{{else}}
### `setPaddleAngle(deg)`
{{/if}}

Sets the deflector's center angle to `deg`, normalized into `[0, 360)`. A
parked ball follows it to the new angle, and the span is untouched.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `launchBall(state)`
{{else}}
### `launchBall()`
{{/if}}

Acts exactly as `Space` does on a parked ball: the parked ball launches
radially outward at the current wave's ball speed. With no parked ball the call
changes nothing.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `clearBalls(state)`
{{else}}
### `clearBalls()`
{{/if}}

Removes every ball, parked included, leaving `balls` empty. Nothing burns up,
so no life is lost, no cue plays, and no particle fires; the field simply
stands without a ball until one is spawned or parked.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `spawnBall(state, x, y, vx, vy)`
{{else}}
### `spawnBall(x, y, vx, vy)`
{{/if}}

Adds one unparked ball at `(x, y)` with velocity `(vx, vy)`, appended in spawn
order. The ball is piercing exactly when `pierceTicks` is above `0` at the
call, and it keeps its posed speed until a rule of
`specs/deflector-and-ball.md` changes it. At the 6-ball cap the call changes
nothing.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `parkBall(state)`
{{else}}
### `parkBall()`
{{/if}}

Parks one ball on the deflector, at the serve position
`specs/deflector-and-ball.md` fixes, following the deflector until launched.
While a parked ball exists, or at the 6-ball cap, the call changes nothing.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `clearTargets(state)`
{{else}}
### `clearTargets()`
{{/if}}

Removes every target from all three rings. It is not a destruction and not a
clearing: nothing scores, no pod draw is made, no cue plays, and the screen
stays where it stands, so a field emptied this way plays on in `playing`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `spawnTarget(state, ring, slot, hp)`
{{else}}
### `spawnTarget(ring, slot, hp)`
{{/if}}

Places a target with `hp` hit points in slot `slot` of ring `ring`, replacing
whatever the slot holds. `ring` is `1` to `3`, `slot` is `0` to that ring's
slot count minus `1`, and `hp` is a whole number of at least `1`. The target
occupies its slot's arc under the ring's current angle, exactly as a wave-start
target does.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setRingAngle(state, ring, deg)`
{{else}}
### `setRingAngle(ring, deg)`
{{/if}}

Sets ring `ring`'s angle to `deg`, normalized into `[0, 360)`. Every target on
the ring moves with it, and the ring's speed is untouched.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setRingSpeed(state, ring, degPerSec)`
{{else}}
### `setRingSpeed(ring, degPerSec)`
{{/if}}

Sets ring `ring`'s orbital speed to `degPerSec`, signed as `specs/rings.md`
signs it. The posed speed holds until the next `setWave` or wave transition
restores the wave formula, and the ring kick of `specs/deflector-and-ball.md`
follows the posed speed at once.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `clearPods(state)`
{{else}}
### `clearPods()`
{{/if}}

Removes every falling pod, leaving `pods` empty. Nothing is caught and nothing
burns, so no score, cue, effect, or particle comes of it.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `spawnPod(state, kind, x, y)`
{{else}}
### `spawnPod(kind, x, y)`
{{/if}}

Adds one pod of `kind`, one of the five kinds `specs/pods.md` names, at
`(x, y)`. It falls radially inward at the fall speed `specs/pods.md` fixes,
from the call onward, and catching or burning it resolves exactly as for a
drawn pod. The generator is
not consumed, so the seeded sequence stays where it stands.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setEffectTicks(state, kind, ticks)`
{{else}}
### `setEffectTicks(kind, ticks)`
{{/if}}

Sets the timer of the timed effect `kind`, one of `widen`, `narrow`, and
`pierce`, to `ticks`, a whole number of at least `0`. A `ticks` above `0` puts
the effect in force exactly as catching its pod would, with the same span
change and the same mutual cancel between `widen` and `narrow`, and the timer
at exactly `ticks`; the catch score is not added. A `ticks` of `0` ends the
effect and restores the baseline exactly as its expiry does.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setShield(state, active)`
{{else}}
### `setShield(active)`
{{/if}}

`setShield(true)` raises the shield ring exactly as catching a shield pod
raises it, and `setShield(false)` removes it. Neither scores, and a raised
shield is consumed by its first reflection exactly as `specs/pods.md` states.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setWaveAdvance(state, on)`, `setPodSpawn(state, on)`
{{else}}
### `setWaveAdvance(on)`, `setPodSpawn(on)`
{{/if}}

Set the two driver switches, as the driver section above describes. Each is on
by default and a `reset` restores both.

## Snapshot shape

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`snapshot` returns:
{{else}}
`snapshot()` returns:
{{/if}}

```js
{
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "playing" | "waveclear" | "paused" | "gameover",
  ticks: <number>,          // ticks resolved since the last reset, on
                            // every screen; frozen screens still count them
  wave: <number>,
  score: <number>,
  lives: <number>,
  seed: <number>,           // the seed the pod generator was laid with
  interstitialTicks: <number>, // ticks left of the interstitial; counts down
                            // on `waveclear` alone
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
  autoStep: <boolean>,      // whether the frame loop advances the simulation
{{/if}}
  waveAdvance: <boolean>,   // driver switch
  podSpawn: <boolean>,      // driver switch
  paddle: { angleDeg: <number>, spanDeg: <number> },
  balls: [                  // spawn order, oldest first
    { x: <number>, y: <number>, vx: <number>, vy: <number>,
      parked: <boolean>, piercing: <boolean> },
  ],
  rings: [                  // entry 0 is ring 1, the innermost
    { angleDeg: <number>, speedDegPerSec: <number>,
      targets: [{ slot: <number>, hp: <number> }] },
  ],
  pods: [                   // spawn order, oldest first
    { kind: <string>, x: <number>, y: <number> },
  ],
  effects: {
    widenTicks: <number>,   // whole ticks remaining; 0 is not in force
    narrowTicks: <number>,
    pierceTicks: <number>,
    shieldActive: <boolean>,
  },
  menu: { index: <number> }, // the highlighted item, 0 on a screen with no menu
}
```

The shape is fixed and every field is present on every screen. `balls` lists
every live ball, the parked ball included, in spawn order. `rings` always holds
three entries, ring 1 first, and each ring's `targets` lists its live targets
by `slot` with the hit points each has left, so a slot with no entry holds no
target. `paddle.angleDeg` and every ring's `angleDeg` are normalized into
`[0, 360)`, and the three effect timers count down in whole ticks. `seed` is
the value the last `reset` laid the pod generator with, so a scenario reads
back the seed it posed.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}

`autoStep` is `true` while the frame loop advances the simulation from the wall
clock, which is how a build starts and how it is played, and `false` while
`setAutoStep(false)` holds the simulation off it.
{{/if}}

Every field is read straight off the game's state, so what the snapshot reports
is what the game holds.

## Diagnostics

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The runtime's debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources.
{{else}}
The engine's debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources.
{{/if}}
Register at least the current `screen`, the score, the lives, the wave, the
deflector's angle and span, the count of live balls, the count of live targets,
the count of falling pods, the three effect timers, and the shield state, the
same facts the snapshot reports. Keep each one short enough to read on a line,
and keep every source a pure read, so watching the overlay leaves the game as
it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources, the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`) shows and hides
it, it is off when the game starts, and it reads the game without changing it.
It is a diagnostic layer rather than part of the game's presentation, so keep
it visually plain and clearly separate from the HUD.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Kessler's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the
read, as `DeepReadonly<KesslerState>`, and reads off that argument. Drawing the
panel, toggling it with the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`),
and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Kessler's part, through
`world.diagnostics` in the game mode's `beginPlay`. Each source is a function
of no arguments, called at each read; read the live state at the call, through
the world the mode holds, so the panel reports the frame being drawn. Drawing
the panel, toggling it with the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code`
`Backquote`), and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{/if}}
